[h1]Phobos Framework[/h1]
[b]Version:[/b] 0.38.0

[h2]Performance update[/h2]
Shared display updates now avoid repeated work, and crew equipment searches stay within their own ship. Optional performance recording remains off unless requested. Saved state and gameplay rules are unchanged.
Offline checks accompany this draft. Measured improvement and in-game interaction still await owner feedback. [url=https://github.com/phobos-dthorga/phobos-ostranauts/blob/main/docs/development/performance-audit.md]Audit and optional playtesting checks[/url].

[b]Publication status:[/b] Not published - development candidate

Version 0.31.1 fixes an error when the game checks an unnamed condition during section selection. Assembly requirements stay unchanged. Offline regression checks pass; live confirmation remains pending.

Shared controls and services for the Phobos mods. Install it with the content mods that need it; Framework is not a ship full of new machinery.

[h2]What it does[/h2]
[list]
[*]Use shared machinery controls, store selection, crew standing orders and training.
[*]Give crew a chosen job and approved supplies. Orders begin disabled; shifts, needs, access and direct orders still matter.
[*]Supported onboard work can run during time-skip. Exterior work and flight pause and need Resume.
[*]Shared translation support uses English when a translation is missing. Completion alerts share one volume control.
[*]Construction, supply transfers and station purchases check materials, space and power. A full destination keeps cargo at its source.
[*]Crew hauling and machine storage routes use one rule for suitable containers: finite, unlocked, and not a person or other equipment.
[*]Automation that switches ship sensors on uses the native Sensors page switch, remembers what it switched and switches off only that. Your own switch always wins. Notices use the crew log and the native nav-map warning banner.
[*]Crew study Phobos specialities at powered terminals through the game's own study actions, and AutoTask crew choose them on their own. Standing orders no longer interrupt study, and a failed step waits before it is retried. Type phobosframework crew in the F3 console to see why someone is or is not studying, and who could take each order step now.
[*]Version 0.36.0 lets the game lead: any crew member the game admits takes an order step (not only your own character), needs are handled by the crew's own pledges rather than a stricter gate, time-skip repairs keep the game's allowance, refused finishes close their task, long unobserved intervals catch up like native machines, and Escape closes a store picker before its panel.
[*]Version 0.38.0 lets standing orders take supplies from anywhere aboard (deck, stores and other machines' trays, nearest first), as the game's own Reload job searches; items on the deck count as cargo in play and in a time-skip.
[/list]

[h2]Operating limits[/h2]
Framework provides common systems, not new recipes for every material. Saved-state faults stop the affected work for inspection. Other mods keep their own inventories and tasks.

[h2]Requirements and installation[/h2]
Ostranauts 1.0.1.5 and BepInEx 5. Content mods specify the Framework version they need. Phobos Scope is a separate optional performance-analysis toolkit.

This is an unpublished development package. Automated checks do not establish in-game readiness or Unity layout approval. Keep required mods installed for saves using their content. Updating does not refill shops or old loot containers. See the installation guide for current download availability.

[h2]English wording update[/h2]
Controls, warnings and descriptions use clearer language. Equipment identities, saved progress, recipes and gameplay values are unchanged.

[h2]Guides, sources and credits[/h2]
Created by Phobos A. D'thorga (phobosgekko). Independent community work, not endorsed by Blue Bottle Games, Kitfox Games or the research institutions cited below. Research informs design; compact equipment, yields and operating limits remain gameplay choices.
The linked guides preserve Blue Bottle Games game evidence and the original research authors beside their claims. Valtora owns the optional Ship's Water integration. LOGUSS's Common Sense modules and jossla's Study at Terminals retain their own work and attribution.
Original artwork uses AI-assisted production; artwork provenance and licensing are recorded separately.

